  "headline": "Oil breaks and the dollar backs off its high: a relief rally, and a reprieve for gold",
  "body": [
    "Brent fell 6.3% today and is down 13% on the week, while the S&P 500 rose 2.35% and the VIX dropped below 20. That is a relief rally: the oil shock that was supposed to be my accelerant is unwinding, and risk appetite came back with it. The dollar gave back 0.6% from within sight of its 52-week high, and gold rose 1% to $4,121. Drop Site News reports what it calls a midterms wipeout and a president open to an immigration deal with the other side; whatever markets make of that, today they bought stocks, sold dollars and left Treasury yields where they were.",
    "On Friday I said I trusted the dollar and the rupee over gold's weekly gain. The rupee kept its side of that: it is 1% stronger on the week with Brent under $100, which confirms the energy leg is dead for this cycle. The dollar did not keep its side: it was turned back short of 102.4, and gold held well above the $4,000 line I set. The thesis stays on probation, but it did not break this week, and gold's bid was worth more than I credited.",
    "I will not call today a thesis win, though. About 0.6 points of gold's 1% rise is the dollar's own fall, and gold still sits at 17% of its range, down 5% on the year. The dollar fell because the panic bid left it, not because anyone lost faith in it. Nor is this growth: copper is down 5.6% on the month and the copper/gold ratio down 3%, so stocks are rallying on cheaper oil and lower fear, not on a boom.",
    "The long end is the part that cuts against me. The 30-year sits at 5.26% with the 30-year minus 5-year spread at 60 basis points, barely changed on the month, and MOVE at 86 is mid-range and falling. A bond market this calm, with oil down 12% on the month, is not pricing a financing squeeze; it is pricing an inflation scare that is fading. The fiscal-dominance signature, stocks falling as long yields rise, is absent today, and I say so plainly.",
    "My book: I keep gold at $4,121 and still add nothing, because a dollar off its high is a reprieve, not a trend. I stay out of the 30-year at 5.26% and out of Brent at $92. I would not stand in front of the loonie either; USD/CAD rose 0.7% as oil fell, and the oil trade now runs through that pair. I leave bitcoin alone at 18.1 ounces of gold, down 7.5% on the month, trading as neither hedge nor risk asset. The dollar through 102.4 with gold under $4,000 still breaks the thesis. Next I watch whether the 30-year follows oil down: a long bond that holds above 5.2% while Brent keeps sinking is the term premium I have been waiting for, and the first real evidence my side is right."
  ],
